That's a hundred faint grey curves, one per simulated dataset, with our real fifteen animals drawn as the single bold orange line, pushed to the background and highlighted deliberately (the same "grey the rest, accent the one thing that matters" move from @sec-eda-ggplot's own figures), applied here to a model's predictions instead of raw data. It's the identical comparison `pp_check(informed_fit, ndraws = 100)` made for you back in @sec-mcmc-model-checking, just assembled from the `ggplot2` primitives this part actually taught you, rather than a function you were asked to trust on faith. The point isn't that the hand-built version is somehow more correct (`pp_check()` was doing the same computation the whole time). It's that you can now see exactly what it was doing.

That's the real argument for treating EDA as a standing habit rather than a chapter you graduate from: `priors-regularization.qmd`'s five-second glance at plausible GFAP values, done "to keep a prior honest," was already EDA, just not yet called by that name or given its own tools. A posterior predictive check is EDA turned on a model's output instead of on raw measurements. Once you've internalized "look before you trust," it doesn't stay confined to the chapter that first taught it to you.

## Opening the door to Part 3

Here's the shape of what changes from here. Everything in this part looked at `cell_data` *before* any model touched it: distributions, relationships, redundancies, all inspected on their own terms. Starting with `linear-regression-what.qmd`, we finally build a model and ask it to commit to a specific claim about those relationships. And the moment a model exists, the questions this part asked informally get formal names and formal tests: is the relationship actually linear (we already have reason to doubt it), is the variance actually constant (we already have reason to doubt that too), are the predictors actually independent of each other (two of ours, we now know precisely, are not). Every "we noticed X" in the table above is about to become "here's the assumption X violates, and here's what we do about it."

I'm not going to open that toolkit here: that's the next chapter's job, not this one's closing paragraph. What I want you carrying forward instead is simpler: nothing in the next part should surprise you. Every crack a regression diagnostic is about to find in `cell_data`, you already saw first, informally, with your own eyes, in this part of the book. That's exactly the order I wanted it in.
